# Kanban OOM: the agent interpreter is the kernel's victim, and the run is booked as a plain crash

Written 2026-11-15 ~01:45Z by chat 20261111_200009_edbe39. All files named below are in this directory.

Problem: measurement card t_265e1af765122e62 counted 0 Kanban runs failed "OOM-killed at" across the hub and
worker-1..8 for 2026-11-13 06:15 to 2026-11-15 00:29 UTC. In that window the kernel journals show 12 OOM kills inside 5
Kanban run scopes (oom-victims.out). In 2 of those runs the victim was the agent's own interpreter, and the run died:
- worker-5, t_06766cf6fff5d7ce, local run 16314 / board run 48611. Spawned launcher 2111911, bound interpreter 2112259.
  Kernel victim: python3 2112259 at 2026-11-14T14:44:03Z, anon-rss 241.559 MiB, MemoryMax 8192 MiB. Booked `crashed`:
  "pid 2111911 exited with code 1 [managed worker lost: EOFError]".
- worker-7, t_ad3f795fa1bbb22f, local run 3383 / board run 44800. Spawned 4172657, bound 4172725. At
  2026-11-14T00:57:40Z the kernel killed children 1898176 and 1669485, then the agent 4172725 (anon-rss 315.328 MiB).
  A child, 2066038, was still alive and was killed at 01:00:21. Booked `crashed`: "managed worker lost: EOFError".
The other 10 kills hit commands. Those runs went on: worker-4 completed, worker-6 blocked normally, and worker-8 ended
rate-limited. So OOMPolicy=continue works when the victim is a command.

Why 1: the measurement counts runs whose error contains "OOM-killed at". Both OOM-killed runs carry
"managed worker lost: EOFError" instead. Evidence: run-histories.txt (shadow `task_runs` 48611 and 44800).

Why 2: the dispatcher calls `read_failure(..., pid=pid)` with the spawned launcher's pid (`tasks.worker_pid`). The
kernel's victim was the bound interpreter, so nothing matches and `read_failure` returns None. Evidence:
`hermes_cli/kanban_db_dispatch.py` ~L1137 (`read_failure(task_id, run_id, log or "", pid=pid, ...)`), and
replay-results.txt. On worker-5 core ac2a498bc2a and worker-7 core 6e5efc06c1c, the same code returns None for the
spawned pid. For the bound pid it returns "Worker was OOM-killed at 8192 MiB (kernel victim: the worker itself, python3
pid 2112259, anon-rss 241.559 MiB) ...".

Why 3: `read_failure`'s docstring says "the run failed by OOM only when that victim is the worker ``pid`` itself (the
agent was the largest process in its scope)". It treats the agent as the spawned pid. Managed workers run the agent in
a separate bound interpreter: spawned ≠ bound in 5346 of 5346 hub runs over 3 days (spawned-vs-bound.txt).

Why 4 (why the agent died at all): commands inherit the agent's oom_score_adj. The bound interpreters run at 200 on the
hub and on worker-3 (adj-and-cgroup.txt). Under the run's MemoryMax the kernel therefore kills the largest process.
When many smaller processes fill the cap, the 241–315 MiB agent is the largest. Nothing in core sets oom_score_adj: a
grep of hermes_cli/, tools/, agent/, gateway/ and plugins/, plus hermes-mods and hermes-state/home/bin, finds no match.
Evidence: kernel-journal-excerpts.txt (victim order), adj-and-cgroup.txt.

Root (happened): the OOMPolicy=continue design (ecf720ae469 and follow-ups ebde9c724a8, a49a5ae38e9, afb3c812fe9) has two
gaps. It identifies the agent by the spawned pid, and it leaves victim choice to RSS alone. When the agent is the
largest process it dies, and its death is booked as a generic crash.

Root (not caught): the OOM tests model the worker as the pid passed to `read_failure`
(tests/hermes_cli/test_kanban_oom_policy_continue.py ~L183-227, `read_failure("t_x", 1, "", pid=999)`). No test has a
spawned pid that differs from the bound pid. The measurement card also trusted the classifier's own string, with no
cross-check against the kernel journal.

Recurrence: first time. t_d394dabe374f50ef (an OOM in a process-registry scope) has no bound-pid or oom_score_adj content.

Effect of the misbooking:
- the retry gets the same MemoryMax instead of the doubled cap that `oom_killed` earns (kanban_worker_scope.py ~L498-522);
- the crash counts toward the failure breaker;
- the kill is missing from OOM counts (OOM_ALERT_THRESHOLD and the measurement).

Countermeasures:
- symptom/classifier: match the kernel victim against the spawned pid plus every `worker_bound` pid of the run.
  Proof: a dispatcher-level test using these pids fails on main and passes on the fix.
- root/victim choice: commands the terminal tool starts inside a Kanban run scope run at oom_score_adj 1000
  (`choom -n 1000 --`). The kernel then kills commands before the agent. Proof: the A/B kernel probe on a pool worker,
  where the parent is the first victim on main and children are the first victims on the fix.
- detection: tests where the spawned pid differs from the bound pid, with fixtures copied verbatim from
  kernel-journal-excerpts.txt. A correction comment on t_265e1af765122e62 gives the real count.

## Proof on worker-8

All 13 source evidence hashes verified. Fixture copied byte-for-byte from kernel-journal-excerpts.txt.
Kernel A/B: 256 MiB scopes, OOMPolicy=continue, parent touches 160 MiB, six children touch 40 MiB each. A parent pid 425295 exited 137; B parent pid 425859 survived and exited 0. B children used choom -n 1000 --. Agent adjustment stayed 200.

